{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_00_Mp2yOXUHTVwO5tZdLgZD2308","type":"function","function":{"name":"run_command","arguments":"{\"command\": \"npm --prefix \\\"AI   文件夹/mind-extension\\\" run lint 2>&1\", \"timeoutSec\": 15}"}}],"reasoning_content":"Also need to apply the same version check for the cache-hit path. Actually, the cache path doesn't need it since it's synchronous. \n\nNow let me also fix Bug 6's debounce side — when the user edits during analysis, we should cancel the running request. But `analyzeContent` is async and uses `fetch` which we can abort. Let me add AbortController tracking.\n\nActually, I already have the version check. The version increment in the debounce callback ensures that when the user edits again during an analysis, the next debounce fires with a new version, and when the old analysis completes, it gets discarded by the version check.\n\nWait, let me re-read the flow:\n\n1. User edits at t=0\n2. Debounce starts (500ms)\n3. At t=500, `triggerAnalysis` is called → `pendingAnalysisVersion` becomes 1 → sends request A\n4. User edits again at t=600\n5. Debounce restarts (500ms)\n6. Request A completes at t=700 → `pendingAnalysisVersion` is still 1 → applied ✓\n7. At t=1100, `triggerAnalysis` is called → `pendingAnalysisVersion` becomes 2 → sends request B\n8. Request B completes at t=1400 → applied ✓\n\nOK, so the version check correctly handles the case. But what about this scenario:\n\n1. Edit at t=0 → debounce 500ms\n2. Edit at t=300 → debounce resets to 500ms (from t=300)\n3. triggerAnalysis at t=800 → version=1 → sends request A\n4. Edit at t=900 → debounce resets to 500ms (from t=900)\n5. Request A completes at t=1200 → version was 1, now `pendingAnalysisVersion` is 2 → discarded ✓\n6. triggerAnalysis at t=1400 → version=2 → sends request B\n7. Request B completes at t=1700 → applied ✓\n\nThis works. The version check correctly discards the stale response.\n\nNow let me verify compilation."}
