e for the last token in the last line. But when this string is inside the PowerShell here-string, `$$r` would be... actually, in the here-string `@\"...\"@`, `$` is not special, so `$$r` stays as literal `$$r`. Then when written to the batch file, it becomes `$$r`. When the batch file runs, the `cmd` passes `$$r` to `powershell -Command`. In PowerShell, `$$` is a special variable (the last token), but `$$r` would be... `$$` followed by `r`, which PowerShell would interpret as `$$` (the last token variable) concatenated with the string `r`. This is not correct.\n\nActually wait, I already know this is a problem. The original batch file I wrote earlier had:\n```\npowershell -Command \"try { $r = Invoke-WebRequest -Uri 'http://localhost:3456/health' -TimeoutSec 3; exit 0 } catch { exit 1 }\" >nul 2>&1\n```\n\nBut in a batch file, `%r%` is variable expansion. But within a string argument to powershell, `$r` is a PowerShell variable. The batch file doesn't touch `$` characters (only `%`). So `$r` in the batch file string is passed literally to PowerShell.\n\nBut wait - I changed it to `$$r` because I thought the PowerShell here-string would interpret `$`. In a PowerShell here-string (`@\"...\"@`), variables ARE NOT expanded. So `$r` stays as literal `$r`. Good.\n\nBut then I wrote `$$r` - which becomes `$$r` in the file. When PowerShell runs this, `$$` is the last token from the last pipeline, and `r` is just a character. So `$$r` evaluates to the last token + \"r\". This is wrong.\n\nLet me simplify. I'll use a different approach - write the batch file with `write_file` but explicitly use `\\r\\n` line endings.\n\nActually, the simplest fix: I'll write the batch file using `write_file` with explicit `\\r\\n` line endings."}
