the bridge, the bridge's analyze endpoint returned empty results, but then when the user started typing or something... No, that doesn't generate PySpark code either.\n\nWait - I think the issue might be simpler. The user might have opened `test.mind` that I just created (which has `# @d` content), and the bridge was running, so the `triggerGeneration` function in extension.ts was called, which asked the AI to generate Python code. The AI might have interpreted the context and generated PySpark code instead of the expected code.\n\nBut the prompt says \"根据 .mind 文件中 # @d 开头的注释...\" so it should generate code based on the # @d comment, which was \"实现两数相加\".\n\nHmm, or maybe the AI just returned something unexpected. Let me check...\n\nActually, I think the real issue is that the user just created a .mind file (maybe the test.mind I created) and WITHOUT any user input, it triggered analysis and code generation. But the user expected that creating an empty file would do nothing.\n\nThe root cause might be:\n1. The `onDidChangeActiveTextEditor` event fires when the user opens a .mind file\n2. It calls `triggerAnalysis` which sends the content to the bridge\n3. If the file has `# @d`, it calls `triggerGeneration` which generates PySpark code\n\nBut this only happens if the file HAS `@d` in it. If the user created an EMPTY file, nothing should generate.\n\nWait, but the test.mind I created DOES have `# @d`. The user might have opened that file, saw the generated PySpark code, and was confused.\n\nActually no, the user said \"一创建mind文件跑出来了这个\". They just created a .mind file and this appeared. The only .mind file I created in the extension directory was test.mind which has # @d content.\n\nLet me just explain to the user what happened clearly."}
