{"role":"assistant","content":"桥接没在跑——所以 `# @d` 没反应（生成依赖桥接），但缩进线应该仍有本地渲染。先启动桥接：","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_00_GzZVAZqSL18Ho8fpcd7a4379","type":"function","function":{"name":"run_background","arguments":"{\"command\": \"node \\\"AI   文件夹/mind-bridge/src/index.js\\\"\", \"waitSec\": 3}"}}],"reasoning_content":"Bridge is not running! That explains why `# @d` does nothing - no generation can happen without the bridge. And the indent lines... let me check if they should still work without the bridge.\n\nLooking at the code flow:\n1. User types in .mind file\n2. `indentDecoration.ts`'s `onDidChangeTextDocument` fires → `renderIndentLines(editor)` → THIS SHOULD WORK without the bridge since it's purely local\n3. On Enter, `extension.ts`'s `onDidChangeTextDocument` fires → `triggerAnalysis` → tries to call bridge → fails silently (returns null or empty)\n\nWait, actually step 3 doesn't fail silently. If `analyzeContent` returns null (bridge not running), the code goes to:\n```typescript\nif (result) {\n  // ... only when bridge works\n} else {\n  statusBarItem.text = '$(warning) Mind (离线)';\n  console.warn('[Mind] 分析请求失败，使用缓存或降级模式');\n}\n```\n\nSo the status bar would show \"(离线)\" and generation wouldn't happen.\n\nBut the indent lines SHOULD still render because `indentDecoration.ts` has its own `onDidChangeTextDocument` handler that calls `renderIndentLines` directly (no bridge needed).\n\nSo for indent lines not working, there might be another issue. Let me check if the indent line decorations are properly initialized.\n\nActually, the indent decorations depend on `TextEditorDecorationType` being created via `rebuildDecorationTypes` which requires the config to be loaded. If the extension isn't properly activated, the decorations won't be created.\n\nLet me start the bridge and also check the indent code.\n\nBut first, let me address both issues:\n1. Bridge not running → start it\n2. Indent lines - let me check the algorithm more carefully\n\nFor the indent lines, the algorithm is:\n1. Parse each line to get indent level (number of leading spaces / 2)\n2. For each level, create a decoration with `├─`, `└─`, or `│`\n3. `isLastChild` determines if it's `├─` or `└─`\n\nLet me think about whether the algorithm correctly handles the case where the user types:\n```\n# @d 你是否能正确工作？\n```\n\nThis line has no leading spaces, so level = 0. For level 0, it uses `contDecos[0]` (the `│ ` decoration). This should show a vertical line at the start of the line.\n\nBut wait - this is a COMMENT line. Comments should probably not get indent lines, or should they? Looking at `computeFallbackNesting` in the bridge:\n```javascript\n// 忽略空行和纯注释行\nif (text.trim() === '' || text.trim().startsWith('#')) continue;\n```\n\nComments are skipped in the nesting calculation. But in `indentDecoration.ts`, the `parseIndents` function doesn't skip comments:\n```typescript\nif (text.trim() === '') {\n  result.push({ level: -1, text: '', isEmpty: true });\n} else {\n  const indentMatch = text.match(/^(\\s*)/);\n  const indentLen = indentMatch ? indentMatch[1].length : 0;\n  result.push({ level: Math.floor(indentLen / 2), text, isEmpty: false });\n}\n```\n\nSo comments ARE getting indent lines. This might be what the user means by \"缩进显示不太对\".\n\nActually, looking at the original requirement document:\n```\n│Count node num\n│if num ≥ 30\n│  ├─ delete minimum num\n│  └─ add node count to total\n│print result\n```\n\nComments should probably get the `│` vertical line too, since they're part of the document structure. But let me focus on what the user is actually seeing.\n\nLet me just start the bridge and let the user test again."}
