{"role":"assistant","content":"明白你的意思了。但是这里有个矛盾需要说清楚：\n\n### 关于\"只提交修改的内容\"\n\n每次分析**必须发完整文件**才能让 AI 理解全部上下文。如果只发修改的部分：\n\n```\n之前: Count items / if count ≥ 5 / print hello\n这次只发: print bye\nAI 会以为文件里只有 \"print bye\" 这一行 → 分析错\n```\n\n所以**必须发全量文件**。但 DeepSeek 有缓存——系统提示词 + 文件前缀相同的部分不走计费，实际改了一行只多花几个 token。\n\n### 关于\"在 reasonix 会话栏目看到\"\n\n这个我能做——Reasonix 的会话存在 `~/.reasonix/sessions/` 目录下。但它的内部格式我不清楚，强行写进去可能不兼容。\n\n不过可以换个思路——**在桥接端开一个 HTTP 接口**，Reasonix 可以直接请求查看所有 `.mind` 的对话记录。或者你想怎么做？我看看 Reasonix 现有能力：","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_00_U2e0MwPxZ2zx5u9wSH8I6753","type":"function","function":{"name":"run_command","arguments":"{\"command\": \"reasonix sessions --help 2>&1\"}"}}],"reasoning_content":"The user wants:\n\n1. The .mind analysis sessions to appear in the Reasonix terminal web interface's session list\n2. Instead of sending the full file content each time, only send the changes (diffs)\n\nThis is a significant feature request. Let me think about how to implement this.\n\nThe user has `reasonix` CLI installed globally. The `reasonix sessions` command lists saved chat sessions, and `reasonix replay` replays transcript files. The sessions are stored in `~/.reasonix/` directory.\n\nFor the sessions to appear in Reasonix's session list, I need to understand how Reasonix stores its sessions. Looking at the earlier help output:\n- `reasonix sessions [options] [name]` - lists saved chat sessions\n- `reasonix replay [options] <transcript>` - replays transcripts\n\nThe user specifically said \"reasonix终端网页\" - Reasonix terminal web interface. This suggests there's a web UI component of Reasonix (running on some port, similar to a dashboard).\n\nTo integrate with Reasonix's session system, I would need to:\n1. Store the analysis conversations in Reasonix's own session format\n2. Register them so `reasonix sessions` can see them\n\nBut I think a more practical approach is:\n1. Save the conversation transcripts as Reasonix-compatible JSONL files in Reasonix's session directory\n2. The user can then use `reasonix replay` to view them\n3. For incremental updates - track what changed between analyses and only send the diff\n\nActually, looking at the Reasonix CLI more carefully, it has `sessions` command that lists sessions. The sessions are likely stored in `~/.reasonix/sessions/` or similar.\n\nBut integrating with Reasonix's session system would require understanding its internal data format, which I don't have access to.\n\nLet me take a different approach. Instead of trying to hack into Reasonix's session system (which I don't have documentation for), let me:\n\n1. Create a bridge-side session system that Reasonix can interact with\n2. Save incremental content changes (diff-based) to reduce token usage\n\nFor the \"only submit modified content\" part - this is actually great for token efficiency. Instead of sending the entire file each time, the plugin could:\n1. Track the last sent content\n2. Compute the diff between the current content and the last sent content\n3. Only send the changes\n\nThis would be more efficient for DeepSeek API since the previous full content is cached, and only the changes need to be processed.\n\nActually wait - DeepSeek's caching works best when the prompt PREFIX is identical. If I send only the diff, the prefix changes completely each time, which would MISS the cache. Sending the full file is more cache-friendly.\n\nBut the user specifically asked for this, so let me implement it their way.\n\nLet me think about what's actually achievable here:\n\n1. For Reasonix session integration: I can save the analysis conversations as Reasonix-compatible format files in a location where the user can access them. But I can't make them appear in the Reasonix web UI session list without understanding Reasonix's internal APIs.\n\n2. For incremental content: I can modify the bridge client to compute and send diffs instead of full content when the change is small relative to the full file.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what the user really wants:\n- They want to see the .mind analysis sessions in Reasonix\n- They want to reduce token consumption by only sending changes\n\nFor #1, the most practical approach is to save the transcripts in a format Reasonix can read. Looking at the `reasonix replay` command, it takes a transcript file path. The transcripts JSONL format is probably similar to what I'm already saving.\n\nFor #2, I can implement an endpoint that accepts diffs (unified diff format) instead of full content. The bridge can reconstruct the full content by applying the diff to the previous content. But this adds complexity.\n\nActually, for the \"submit only changed content\" approach, the simplest implementation:\n1. Keep the previous content on the server side (bridge)\n2. Client sends the diff (or just the new/changed lines)\n3. Bridge applies the diff to reconstruct full content\n4. Bridge analyzes as before\n\nOr even simpler - since the full file approach is more cache-friendly at DeepSeek's API level, maybe the user's concern is about the HTTP request size, not the token cost. The HTTP request with the full file is small (just a few KB) compared to the DeepSeek API tokens (thousands of tokens).\n\nLet me just tell the user honestly what's practical and what's not, and implement what I can."}
